<worksheet xml:id="ws-4">
     <title>Taylor Series</title>
      <exercise workspace="2.0in">
        <p>
          Use the definition of the Taylor series to find the coefficients of the Taylor series for <m>\cos(x)</m> centered at 
          <m>x=0</m>. Once you recognize a pattern, write down a formula for the power series of <m>\cos(x)</m>
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="2.0in">
        <p>
          Repeat the previous exercise to find the Taylor series for <m>\sin(x)</m>
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="3.0in">
        <p>
          Use Euler's formula: <m>e^{ix} = \cos(x) + i\sin(x)</m> to write down a formula for the Taylor series of
          <m>e^x</m> centered at <m>x=0</m>. 
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="3.0in">
        <p>
          Finally, modify your formula from the previous exercise to write down a formula for the Taylor series of the function
          <m>f(x) = x^4e^{-3x^2}</m> centered at <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
</worksheet>